<Project>

  <!--
    THE ONE BUILD EXTENSION POINT, AND IT IS EMPTY HERE.

    build/Commercial.targets does not exist in this repository and is listed in .gitignore, so the
    condition below is FALSE for every clone, every CI run and every release build of this repository.
    Nothing is imported, nothing changes, and the build is byte-for-byte what it was before this file
    existed. A clone of this repository builds and runs on its own, with no missing file and no unmet
    condition - that is a hard requirement, not a convenience.

    What it is for: a separate build may place its own targets file at that path to add project
    references of its own and to supply the single optional `AddCommercialModules` partial declared in
    App.xaml.cs. The extension point is one-way by construction. This file names no assembly, no type
    and no repository outside this tree; it declares a hook, not a reference, so nothing here can create
    a dependency from this repository onto anything else.

    Enforced, not documented (CommunityBoundaryGuardTests):
      * no TRACKED file may exist at the overlay path - .gitignore alone does not stop `git add -f`;
      * this Import must stay CONDITIONAL, and it is the ONLY import in the tracked tree allowed to
        reference a path that is not present;
      * the guard is an ALLOWLIST over structure. It deliberately does not carry a list of forbidden
        proprietary names, because publishing that list here would itself be the leak it aims to prevent.
  -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)build\Commercial.targets"
          Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)build\Commercial.targets')" />

</Project>
